PART 22 — FAILURE RECOVERY AND PROJECT REPAIR LOOP

Whenever a development, build, test, or deployment failure occurs, follow this sequence:

Step 1 — Capture the evidence.

Read the real error output, logs, failing command, or reproduced behavior.

Step 2 — Classify the problem.

Determine whether it is caused by source code, dependency versions, provider configuration, credentials, network access, storage, runtime compatibility, or deployment configuration.

Step 3 — Identify the root cause.

Do not assume that the first visible error is the underlying problem.

Step 4 — Make the smallest safe correction.

Avoid unrelated rewrites and destructive changes.

Step 5 — Rerun the failed operation.

Verify whether the correction worked.

Step 6 — Run regression checks.

Ensure the correction did not break other features.

Step 7 — Document unresolved blockers.

If the failure depends on unavailable credentials, permissions, external infrastructure, or an inaccessible provider, explain exactly what remains.

If the same repair fails repeatedly, do not blindly repeat it. Reassess the evidence and choose a different justified approach.

